WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:15.940
<v Ira Glass>It's hard to remember now just how often we were hearing about them. How they were being blamed for the entire economic mess, collapsing all around us. And I think it's hard to remember just what a big deal they were, because we never quite understood, not then, not now, what they are.
<v Ira Glass>现在很难回想起来，当时我们听到有关它们的消息是多么频繁。听到它们如何被归咎于在我们周围全面崩溃的整个经济烂摊子。而且我认为很难回想起来它们曾经是多大的事情，因为我们从来没有完全理解——当时没有，现在也没有——它们究竟是什么。

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.710
<v Man 1>The treasury launched the TARP last fall to help rid the nation's banks of toxic assets.
<v Man 1>财政部去年秋天启动了TARP计划，以帮助清理国家各家银行的不良资产。

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.090
<v Man 2>These toxic assets are the cancer in the balance sheet.
<v Man 2>这些不良资产是资产负债表上的毒瘤。

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:27.440
<v Man 3>The President opened up by talking about the importance of dealing with toxic assets.
<v Man 3>总统在开场白中谈到了处理不良资产的重要性。

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.530
<v Barack Obama>Banks aren't the only institutions affected by these toxic assets.
<v Barack Obama>银行并不是唯一受到这些不良资产影响的机构。

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:32.430
<v Woman 1>Toxic assets.
<v Woman 1>不良资产。

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.570
<v Man 4>Toxic assets.
<v Man 4>不良资产。

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:39.480
<v Man 5>Toxic assets.
<v Man 5>不良资产。

00:00:39.480 --> 00:01:01.890
<v Ira Glass>Our Planet Money team has been covering the financial crisis for two years, and they felt like one way to understand what is happening in our economy-- right now, today-- was to become intimately acquainted with the source of its demise. The toxic asset. And so back in January, reporters David Kestenbaum and Chana Joffe-Walt convinced three of their colleagues that they should purchase one. Purchase one for themselves, pitch in together $1,000.
<v Ira Glass>我们的“星球金钱”团队两年来一直在报道这场金融危机，他们觉得理解我们经济中正在发生的事情——此时此刻，今天——的一个方法，就是与经济衰退之源建立亲密的接触。也就是不良资产。因此，早在今年1月，记者大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）和查娜·约菲-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）说服了他们的三位同事，让他们应该买一个。为自己买一个，大家凑齐1000美元。

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.090
<v Man 6>All right, so how much do we owe you?
<v Man 6>你能看出邮政编码。

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:06.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>All right, you guys each owe me $202. Pay up.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好了，你们每人欠我202美元。拿来吧。

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.370
<v Caitlin Kenney>No change? Even $202?
<v Caitlin Kenney>没有找零？连202美元也要？

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:08.870
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>$202.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>$202.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.910
<v Man 6>There's no change.
<v Man 6>没有找零。

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:21.520
<v Ira Glass>That's right, buy one of the things that nearly brought down the global financial system. That blew up major Wall Street banks, that plunged the world into an economic collapse from which it is still struggling to recover. They wanted to buy one of those.
<v Ira Glass>没错，买下几乎导致全球金融体系崩溃的东西之一。买下那些引爆华盛顿大型银行、将世界拖入经济崩溃至今仍在艰难复苏的东西。他们想买其中一个。

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Caitlin, you're dragging over there.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>凯特琳，你在那边磨蹭什么呢。

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:37.480
<v Caitlin Kenney>I'm writing a check, I have to be precise. I'm putting toxic asset as the memo on my check.
<v Caitlin Kenney>我在开支票，我必须写得精确。我在支票的备注栏里写上了“不良资产”。

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:54.030
<v Ira Glass>Today we're going to be hearing the surprisingly riveting tale of a single toxic asset. The Planet Money team has rolled out installments of the story one at a time over the course of the last year, on the Planet Money podcasts and on some of the NPR news shows-- serialized like a Dicken's novel, published one chapter at a time.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们将听到一个关于单一不良资产的、出人意料引人入胜的故事。“星球金钱”团队在过去的一年里，在“星球金钱”播客和一些NPR新闻节目中，陆陆续续推出了这个故事的各个部分——像狄更斯的小说一样连载，一次发表一个章节。

00:01:54.030 --> 00:02:09.260
<v Ira Glass>But today for the first time, we bring you the whole story. Great Expectations , Little Dorrit, Bleak House, the full novel, beginning to end, in one place for the first time, so you can actually follow the entire tale. And to kick us off, I am now joined in the studio by David and Chana.
<v Ira Glass>但今天，我们第一次为您带来完整的故事。《远大前程》、《小杜丽》、《荒凉山庄》，整部小说，从头到尾，破天荒地汇集在一个地方，这样你就可以真正跟随整个故事的发展了。为了拉开序幕，大卫和查娜现在和我一起在演播室里。

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:09.760
<v Ira Glass>Hello.
<v Ira Glass>大家好。

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Hello.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你好。

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:20.500
<v David Kestenbaum>Hey, Ira. All right, so I brought it in. Here it is, this is what we bought. That's chapter one, volume two.
<v David Kestenbaum>嘿，伊拉。好了，所以我把它带来了。就是这个，这就是我们买的。那是第一章，第二卷。

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.220
<v Ira Glass>It's just a pile of papers.
<v Ira Glass>这只是一堆纸。

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:39.130
<v David Kestenbaum>Written by a lot of lawyers, yeah. So technically, this thing is called a mortgage backed security. Basically, a bunch of Wall Street guys grabbed up thousands of mortgages from all over the country, threw them in a pile, and sold off pieces to investors. So big pension funds bought them, insurance funds bought them, banks bought them.
<v David Kestenbaum>由很多律师撰写的，是的。所以严格来说，这东西被称为抵押贷款支持证券。基本上，一帮华盛顿的人从全国各地收集了成千上万笔抵押贷款，把它们扔成一堆，然后把份额卖给投资者。于是大型养老基金买下了它们，保险基金买下了它们，银行买下了它们。

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.250
<v Ira Glass>And you guys bought them?
<v Ira Glass>然后你们买下了它们？

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:50.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well that part happened much later. We bought ours in January of this year, and by that point, things had degraded so much that they were so cheap even a bunch of reporters could afford one.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯，那部分发生得很晚。我们是在今年1月买的我们的那份，到那时，情况已经恶化得如此严重，以至于它们变得非常便宜，甚至一帮记者也能买得起。

00:02:50.680 --> 00:03:17.760
<v Ira Glass>And your goal in buying this toxic asset-- I thought of it as somebody who's a fan of these stories when I would hear them-- you're like archaeologists in this project. You're like forensic doctors trying to reconstruct how the housing market collapsed and the economy died, and truly understand what happened by carefully picking apart the corpse of one financial instrument. And I remember at the beginning, your hope was that this one toxic asset would be like an encyclopedia of the entire financial crisis.
<v Ira Glass>而你们购买这个不良资产的目标——当我听到这些故事时，我把它想象成这些故事的粉丝——你们在这个项目中就像考古学家。你们就像法医，试图通过仔细剖析一个金融工具的尸体，来重构房地产市场是如何崩溃的以及经济是如何消亡的，并真正理解发生了什么。我记得在开始时，你们的希望是，这个单一的不良资产将成为整个金融危机的百科全书。

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:31.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, it has. This thing has everything. There are homeowners and foreclosure, there is crime, there are the unintended consequences of bailing out both huge corporations and individual homeowners. There are questions of morality.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的，确实如此。这东西应有尽有。有房主和止赎，有犯罪，有对拯救大企业和个人房主这两者带来的意想不到的后果。还有道德问题。

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.690
<v David Kestenbaum>And there's a dog named Muffin.
<v David Kestenbaum>还有一只名叫马芬的狗。

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.590
<v Ira Glass>All right, well let's get to it. Our show in five acts.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那我们开始吧。我们的节目分五幕。

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:48.610
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Fire Sale in Kansas City. Take it away.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，堪萨斯城的跳楼大甩卖。开始吧。

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:59.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So we've got our $1,000, we've got the will. Now we just have to buy one. But it turns out, it's not so easy to buy a toxic asset. You kind of need to know a guy. But, David, you knew a guy.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我们有了1000美元，我们有了意愿。现在我们只需要买一个。但事实证明，买一个不良资产并不那么容易。你差不多需要认识某个人。但是，大卫，你认识某个人。

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:14.590
<v David Kestenbaum>Wit Solberg, he used to work on Wall Street, now he'd set up shop in Kansas City, his hometown. He was helping small community banks that got stuck with toxic assets. So I called him, and I said, "How can we buy one of these things?" And Wit said, "I can help you. Come on down." So we did.
<v David Kestenbaum>维特·索尔伯格（Wit Solberg），他过去在华盛顿工作，现在他在他的家乡堪萨斯城开店。他一直在帮助那些陷入不良资产困境的小型社区银行。所以我打电话给他，我说：“我们怎么才能买到这些东西之一呢？”维特说：“我可以帮你们。过来吧。”于是我们就去了。

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.310
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是？

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.490
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. Hey, Chana?
<v David Kestenbaum>对。嘿，查娜？

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:19.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>怎么了？

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.040
<v David Kestenbaum>I forgot my check book.
<v David Kestenbaum>我忘带支票簿了。

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.000
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I got you.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我帮你。

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:23.500
<v David Kestenbaum>All right.
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧。

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:35.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So David, you took me to Kansas City to what feels like an abandoned alley. There's no one around, it's like an unmarked brick building. I feel like we're buying drugs.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以大卫，你把我带到了堪萨斯城一个感觉像是废弃小巷的地方。周围没有人，就像一栋没有标志的砖砌建筑。我觉得我们是在买毒品。

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.870
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, this is really it? Just in one of these little buildings?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，这真的是这里吗？就在这些小建筑里之一？

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.450
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, it used to be a barber shop I think.
<v David Kestenbaum>对，我想它以前是一家理发店。

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:58.130
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So we go upstairs and it looks more legit inside. There are maybe a dozen guys in a big open office, white boards with numbers and lists. And each desk has several computer monitors sort of floating on a sea of crap-- potato chip bags, Snapple bottles, toothpaste, chewing tobacco.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我们上楼去，里面看起来更正规一些。一个大的开放式办公室里大概有十几个小伙子，白板上写着数字和列表。每个桌子上都有几个电脑显示器，仿佛漂浮在一片垃圾的海洋上——薯片袋、Snapple饮料瓶、牙膏、嚼烟。

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.050
<v David Kestenbaum>Chewing tobacco that gets spit out into the Snapple bottle.
<v David Kestenbaum>嚼烟吐在Snapple瓶子里。

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:14.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That would be Wit. Wit Solberg. He's got a blond buzz cut, he's very charming and completely all over the place. Wit is always telling you several things at once. Just getting him to sit down and say his name is tough.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那一定就是维特了。维特·索尔伯格。他留着金色的平头，非常有魅力而且整个人风风火火。维特总是同时跟你说好几件事情。光是让他坐下来报上他的名字都很困难。

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.060
<v Wit Solberg>OK, so we--
<v Wit Solberg>好吧，所以我们——

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What's your name?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你叫什么名字？

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:23.050
<v Wit Solberg>Oh, OK, that's a good point. So my name's Wit.
<v Wit Solberg>哦，好吧，这问得好。所以我的名字叫维特。

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:32.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>OK, so a bit about what we're buying. A long time ago, before toxic assets were toxic, they were called-- and you've heard this phrase-- mortgaged backed securities.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好吧，关于我们正在购买的东西。很久以前，在不良资产还不是不良资产的时候，它们被称为——你听过这个词——抵押贷款支持证券。

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:48.800
<v David Kestenbaum>What that means is that these are thousands of mortgages all bundled together. And every month when people send in their mortgage payments, think of that money as going into one big pot. And then the money in the pot gets used to pay the investors, the owners of this thing. They each get a monthly payment.
<v David Kestenbaum>这意味着这是成千上万笔打包在一起的抵押贷款。每个月当人们寄来他们的抵押贷款月供时，可以把这笔钱想象成进入了一个大池子。然后池子里的钱被用来支付给投资者，即这个东西的所有者。他们每个人都会得到每月的付款。

00:05:48.800 --> 00:06:03.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Of course, we all know what happened. People stopped paying their mortgages. And the amount of money coming in started to dwindle. So there was no way investors are going to get all their money back. At this point, the mortgage backed security has officially become a toxic asset.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当然，我们都知道发生了什么。人们停止支付他们的抵押贷款。进来的资金数额开始萎缩。因此，投资者绝不可能收回他们的全部资金。此时，抵押贷款支持证券已经正式变成了不良资产。

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:15.740
<v David Kestenbaum>And now, lots of investors are trying to unload these things at steep discounts, which is a buying opportunity for us. We want to make some money on this. And we can, if we pick the right one at the right price.
<v David Kestenbaum>现在，许多投资者正试图以巨额折扣甩手这些东西，这对我们来说是一个买入良机。我们想从中赚点钱。如果我们以正确的价格挑选正确的商品，我们就能做到。

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:38.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So we're sitting here in Wit's office about to start bidding on toxic assets that people are trying to dump. Wit's going to look at the toxic assets, analyze them, and help us choose what to buy. He's got three computer monitors on his desk, and brokers send him emails about what toxic assets are for sale today. Only they don't call them toxic assets, they call them bonds. Wit gets lots of emails from guys in New York.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我们坐在维特的办公室里，准备开始竞标人们试图抛售的不良资产。维特将查看不良资产，对其进行分析，并帮助我们选择买什么。他的桌上有三个电脑显示器，经纪人给他发电子邮件，告知今天有哪些不良资产在出售。只是他们不叫它们不良资产，他们叫它们债券。维特收到了很多来自纽约小伙子们的电子邮件。

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:48.300
<v Wit Solberg>Bobby sends a lot of email. And Cliff, and Dwayne, and Adam, and Cliff again, and Chris, and, you know.
<v Wit Solberg>鲍比发了很多电子邮件。还有克利夫、德韦恩、亚当，又是克利夫，还有克里斯，你知道的。

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>These are guys that you trust?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这些是你信任的人吗？

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.290
<v Wit Solberg>They're guys that sell bonds.
<v Wit Solberg>他们是卖债券的人。

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:56.860
<v David Kestenbaum>The emails have subject lines like, super senior steal. One says, cheaper.
<v David Kestenbaum>电子邮件的主题行诸如“超级高级大漏”。其中一封说“更便宜”。

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:16.780
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It takes us a long time to even find a bond Wit wants to make an offer on. But finally, he picks one he thinks looks good. And then he asks this guy Rob-- who kind of looks like he stuck his finger in a light socket-- to go and analyze it with a computer model in the back room. Actually, what Wit specifically says is, "Hey Rob, go back to the bond cave and check this out."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们花了很长时间才找到一个维特想要出价的债券。但最后，他挑了一个他认为看起来不错的。然后他问这个叫罗布的家伙——他看起来就像把手指插进了灯泡插座里——去后屋用计算机模型分析一下。实际上，维特具体说的是：“嘿，罗布，回债券洞穴去查一下这个。”

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:41.420
<v David Kestenbaum>And back in the bond cave, Rob is going to run a bunch of fancy software to try and answer one deceptively simple question. How much should we pay for this bond? Remember, the bond is a pile of mortgages. Imagine that pile. The value of the pile depends on the health of those mortgages. As people's houses get foreclosed on, repossessed, and sold for a loss, pop, one of the mortgages disappears. The pile shrinks.
<v David Kestenbaum>在债券洞穴里，罗布将运行一堆高级软件，试图回答一个看似简单的问题。我们应该为这个债券付多少钱？记住，债券是一堆抵押贷款。想象一下那一堆。这堆东西的价值取决于这些抵押贷款的健康状况。随着人们的房子被止赎、收回并折价出售，啪，其中一笔抵押贷款消失了。这堆东西缩水了。

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:53.420
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Right now, whoever owns the bond that Rob is looking at is receiving monthly payments. But once that pile of mortgages shrinks enough, those payments will stop. This bond will die.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在，罗布正在看的债券的拥有者正在收到每月的付款。但是一旦这堆抵押贷款缩水到一定程度，这些付款就会停止。这个债券将消亡。

00:07:53.420 --> 00:08:04.220
<v David Kestenbaum>So Rob is trying to figure out, if we spend $1,000 to own a piece of this bond now, will those payments continue long enough for us to make our money back, plus some.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以罗布正试图弄清楚，如果我们现在花1000美元拥有这个债券的一部分，这些付款会持续足够长的时间让我们收回本金外加一些利润吗？

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:28.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Rob comes back and says, "Yeah, this one could be OK." And then Wit says something that blows me away. He says we should bid half a cent on the bond. As in one half of one penny on the dollar. Meaning at some point, probably the height of the bubble, say this was worth a full dollar, now we're going to offer to buy it for half a cent-- less than one percent of what someone originally bought it for.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>罗布回来后说：“是的，这个可能还可以。”然后维特说了一句话，把我惊呆了。他说我们应该为这债券出价半个美分。也就是一美元中的半分钱。这意味着在某个时刻，可能是在泡沫的顶峰，假设这曾经值整整一美元，现在我们出价半美分买下它——不到当初别人购买价格的百分之一。

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:46.120
<v David Kestenbaum>So Wit calls Cliff, one of those brokers who sent him the emails. And he says, "Hey man, half a cent on the bond." That's the way these guys talk when they're bidding on something. Two cents, mid teens, twenties. So Cliff says, OK, let me check with whoever's selling it. We hang up, wait, and wait. And finally, Cliff calls back.
