<v David Kestenbaum>At some point, I realized I was the only one clapping, so I stopped. But I am feeling good about our investment. I figured, everyone's afraid of these things, only a handful of people are analyzing them and looking for bargains. We got it for 99% off. We are definitely going to make money. I felt confident.
<v David Kestenbaum>在某个时刻，我意识到我是唯一一个鼓掌的人，所以我停了下来。但我对我们的投资感觉很好。我想，每个人都害怕这些东西，只有少数人在分析它们并寻找便宜货。我们以9%的折扣买下了它。我们绝对会赚钱的。我感到很自信。

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:32.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We all did.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们都这么觉得。

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:50.350
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, An Old Man Chooses Between Logic and Morals, Logic Wins. On January 22, David and Chana took their toxic asset back to the New York office, introduced it around to their colleagues, created a place on the corner window sill for the stack of paperwork. And then stared at it, flipped through it.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，一个老人在逻辑和道德之间做出选择，逻辑赢了。1月22日，大卫和查娜把他们的不良资产带回纽约办公室，向同事们介绍了一番，在角落的窗台上为这叠文件腾出了一个地方。然后盯着它看，翻阅着它。

00:12:50.350 --> 00:13:12.170
<v Ira Glass>They considered some questions that in the end took them eight months to answer. What did they just buy? Who did they buy it from? Whose mortgages were actually in it? It was time to begin their archaeological investigation of their own toxic asset. Somewhere inside there, there were real people, real homeowners, with real houses. The stack of 300 pages did not identify who those real people were.
<v Ira Glass>他们考虑了一些问题，这些问题最终花费了他们八个月的时间来回答。他们刚刚买的是什么？他们是从谁那里买的？里面到底是谁的抵押贷款？是时候对他们自己的不良资产进行考古调查了。在其中的某个地方，有真实的人，真正的房主，拥有真正的房子。这300页的文件并没有指明这些真正的人是谁。

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:21.240
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's actually kind of fun having a toxic asset. David set up a live toxic asset web cam so he could stare at it all the time, just gaze at the stack of paper.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>拥有一个不良资产其实还挺有趣的。大卫设置了一个不良资产实时网络摄像头，这样他就可以一直盯着它，只要凝视着那叠纸就行。

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:26.420
<v David Kestenbaum>I thought other people might want to see it too. Sometimes I'd sit things on it, like flowers, or a bottle of Tylenol.
<v David Kestenbaum>我以为其他人可能也想看看它。有时我会在上面放些东西，比如鲜花，或者一瓶泰诺。

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:31.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We go to a toxic asset investor conference, we start reading obscure trade publications.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们去参加不良资产投资者会议，我们开始阅读冷门的行业出版物。

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:41.860
<v David Kestenbaum>And the toxic asset starts to feel like a pet. We ask our listeners what we should call it and it gets a name, Toxie. And it gets a gender-- we all start referring to it as she for some reason.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个不良资产开始感觉像是一个宠物。我们问听众我们该叫它什么名字，于是它有了一个名字，托克茜（Toxie）。它有了一个性别——不知为什么，我们所有人开始用“她”来指代它。

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:57.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Which always seemed completely unfair to me, since the one thing we can be almost 100% sure of is that this thing was created by men. But whatever, yes, it's nice that people start to connect with her slash it. One listener wrote a song for Toxie.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这对我来说一直显得极其不公平，因为我们几乎能100%确定的唯一一件事是，这东西是由男人创造的。但不管怎样，是的，人们开始和她——或者说它——产生联系，这挺好的。一位听众为托克茜写了一首歌。

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:17.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[MUSIC - "THE TOXIE SONG" BY JAMES GAMMON]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[音乐 - 詹姆斯·加蒙演唱的《托克茜之歌》]

