00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:41.750
<v David Kestenbaum>What's so awkward about this is this is probably the first time someone who invested in a toxic asset, who actually owned a homeowner's mortgage as part of one of these massive bonds, actually tried to get in touch with the homeowners. And think about what that means. The genius of these mortgage backed securities was that they connected people with money to people who wanted to borrow. This happen to the tune of $3 trillion. Hundreds of thousands of investors, millions of home buyers, but the two sides never met. Until now.
<v David Kestenbaum>这最尴尬的地方在于，这可能是投资不良资产的人——真正作为这些巨额债券的一部分而拥有房主抵押贷款的人——第一次真正试图与房主取得联系。想想这意味着什么。这些抵押贷款支持证券的天才之处在于，它们将有钱人与想要借钱的人联系起来。这笔交易额高达3万亿美元。数十万投资者，数百万购房者，但双方从未见面。直到现在。

00:16:41.750 --> 00:17:01.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eventually, we found a homeowner, Richard Koenig. And this was very exciting. Half the homeowners in our toxic asset had stopped paying their mortgages-- half-- and Richard Koenig was one of them--he was not paying us. When we got him on the phone, he said, "Sure, come on down." So we flew to Sarasota to meet him and drove to his house.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>最终，我们找到了一位房主，理查德·科尼格（Richard Koenig）。这非常令人兴奋。我们不良资产中的一半房主已经停止支付房贷——一半——而理查德·科尼格就是其中之一——他没有付钱给我们。当我们通过电话联系到他时，他说：“当然，过来吧。”于是我们飞往萨拉索塔去见他，并开车去了他的房子。

