<v David Kestenbaum>And he has a dog named Muffin.
<v David Kestenbaum>他还有一只名叫马芬的狗。

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:44.000
<v Richard Koenig>My baby. I love my baby.
<v Richard Koenig>我的宝贝。我爱我的宝贝。

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:59.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I know some dog lovers, I have never met somebody as in love with their dog as Richard is with Muffin. So Richard Koenig took us to the actual house that is in our toxic asset, the one that we've been looking at on this spreadsheet. We got to see it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我认识一些爱狗人士，但我从未见过像理查德爱马芬这样深爱自己狗的人。所以理查德·科尼格带我们去了那个在我们不良资产中的真正的房子，也就是我们在电子表格上一直看到的那个房子。我们亲眼看到了它。

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh, it's nice.
<v David Kestenbaum>哦，这很不错。

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Very nice.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>非常好。

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:06.290
<v Richard Koenig>You cannot believe what was done to this place.
<v Richard Koenig>你简直不敢相信这个地方被弄成了这样。

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:17.670
<v David Kestenbaum>Maybe when you think about toxic assets you picture rundown homes falling apart, overgrown lawns. Uh-uh, this place is really lovely. It's a condo on a little pond in a tidy housing development.
<v David Kestenbaum>也许当你想到不良资产时，你会脑补出破旧不堪、杂草丛生的破房子。才不呢，这个地方真的很可爱。这是一个位于整洁住宅区一个小池塘边的公寓。

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:29.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's Richard second home. He planned to move out of his bigger place with his wife and into the condo. That'd be more manageable as he grows older. So in 2005, Richard took out a $300,000 mortgage.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是理查德的第二套房。他计划和妻子搬出较大的房子，搬进这套公寓。随着年龄增长，这会更好打理。所以在2005年，理查德贷款了30万美元。

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:39.520
<v David Kestenbaum>And then the housing market fell apart, and Richard found he owed more on this house than it's worth. So he decided to stay in the house he already lives in and walk away from the condo by the pond.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后房地产市场崩溃了，理查德发现这套房子的欠款超过了它的价值。因此他决定继续住在自己现在住的房子里，而放弃池塘边的这套公寓。

00:18:39.520 --> 00:19:03.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>After Richard showed us his real live house, we showed him what it looked like from our end. There he is just this little line in our spreadsheet that reads, CCC36999. And that nine stands for 90 days delinquent or more, so a bunch of nines in a row means he hasn't been paying his mortgage for a long time. So we show him all of the toxic asset paperwork, all 300 pages.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在理查德向我们展示了他的现实生活中的房子之后，我们向他展示了它在我们这一端的样子。他只是我们电子表格里的一行字，上面写着CCC36999。那个9代表逾期90天或更长时间，所以一连串的9意味着他已经很长时间没有支付房贷了。于是我们把所有的不良资产文件都拿给他看，整整300页。

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.670
<v David Kestenbaum>This is what it looks like. This is where your mortgage ended up.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是它的样子。这就是你的房贷最终的去向。

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:17.940
<v Richard Koenig>Unbelievable, isn't it? My God. My eyes couldn't accommodate it. I'm not going to live long enough to go through that pile of-- pile of poop.
<v Richard Koenig>难以置信，对吧？天哪。我的眼睛可受不了这个。我活不到去翻阅那堆——那堆废纸的时候了。

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:24.170
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did you have a picture of us? Like when you were writing your checks, who you were sending them to?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你对我们有个画面吗？就像你写支票的时候，你在把钱寄给谁？

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:36.950
<v Richard Koenig>Oh, you mean at the bank. No. No, to me, banks-- unless I know the individual, a bank is a blank face. It's a non-living creature.
<v Richard Koenig>哦，你是说在银行。没有。不，对我来说，银行——除非我认识个人，否则银行就是一张空白的脸。它是一个没有生命的生物。

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:39.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Of course, it wasn't a bank, or a non-living creature.
<v David Kestenbaum>当然，它不是一家银行，也不是一个没有生命的生物。

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:41.430
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You were writing checks to us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你是在给我们写支票。

00:19:41.430 --> 00:20:09.410
<v Richard Koenig>Yeah, but not you personally. Now, had you been the recipients on a personal basis, after having met you, I'd write you the check. How's that? And do I feel sorry for the investors? Sure I do. I'm a sensitive person, you know? I really am. I love my wife, I love my dog, I love my children and my grandkids. Yeah, I'd feel badly.
<v Richard Koenig>是的，但不是针对你个人。现在，如果你是以个人的身份作为收款人，在见过你之后，我会把支票写给你。怎么样？我为投资者感到难过吗？我当然难过。我是一个敏感的人，你知道吗？我真的是。我爱我的妻子，我爱我的狗，我爱我的孩子和孙子孙女。是的，我会觉得不好受。

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:10.870
<v David Kestenbaum>We don't hold it against you.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们不怪你。

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:13.000
<v Richard Koenig>Good. Please don't.
<v Richard Koenig>很好。请别怪我。

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:30.900
<v David Kestenbaum>And really, I don't. Richard is under water on the house. He owes more than it's worth, and he has a home, so he can walk away from this second house. It's going to kill his perfect credit rating, but he doesn't really need good credit anymore. He's 81 years old, he's probably never going to need to borrow money again. So it makes sense for him to just walk away.
<v David Kestenbaum>真的，我没有。理查德的房子资不抵债。他欠的钱比房子本身的价值还要多，而且他还有别的住所，所以他可以放弃这第二套房子。这会毁掉他完美的信用评级，但他其实已经不需要好信用了。他81岁了，可能再也不需要借钱了。所以对他来说，直接放弃是合情合理的。

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:41.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well, maybe logically it makes sense, but he did make a commitment. He gave his word. That's not nothing, that's what it means to be an adult, someone gives you money, you pay it back.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯，从逻辑上讲这也许说得通，但他确实作出了承诺。他许下了诺言。这并非无足轻重，这就是作为一个成年人的意义，别人给你钱，你就要还。

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:48.970
<v David Kestenbaum>And if the value of his house kept going up, he probably would have continued to pay it back. That would have been the logical thing to do. But the logic changed.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果他房子的价值一直在上涨，他可能就会继续还款。那将是符合逻辑的做法。但逻辑变了。

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:51.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So his morals changed too.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以他的道德观念也变了。

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:56.300
<v David Kestenbaum>Was it a hard emotional decision to walk away from that house?
<v David Kestenbaum>放弃那套房子是一个艰难的情感决定吗？

00:20:56.300 --> 00:21:24.910
<v Richard Koenig>Well, yeah, it went against everything that I was ever taught to believe. You know, you just don't turn your back and say, goodbye, good luck. So from that perspective, yes, it did bother me. But if I have to compromise the way I live, I'm really not going to do that. Not at almost 82 years old. I really had no choice.
<v Richard Koenig>嗯，是的，这违背了我被教导要相信的一切。你知道，你不能就这样转过身去说，再见，祝你好运。所以从这个角度来看，是的，这确实困扰了我。但如果我必须妥协我的生活方式，我真的不会那样做。在快82岁的时候更是如此。我真的别无选择。

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:28.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well, you did have a choice. You could have kept paying it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯，你确实有选择。你本可以继续付款的。

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:44.990
<v Richard Koenig>I could have kept paying for it? Not really. You know, at my age, to have payments like that, and a large income no longer a large income, it didn't make any sense at all.
<v Richard Koenig>我本可以继续付款？不见得。你知道，在我的年纪，要背负这样的付款，而且高收入已不复存在，这简直毫无意义。

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:50.580
<v David Kestenbaum>There are lots of Richards these days. In fact, the moral compass of the entire country is shifting.
<v David Kestenbaum>这年头有很多像理查德这样的人。事实上，整个国家的道德罗盘正在发生转变。

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:58.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So much so that there is a name for what Richard is doing. Walking away from your home like this? It's called a strategic default.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>以至于理查德正在做的事情都有了一个专有名称。像这样抛弃你的家？这被称为战略性违约。

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:06.890
<v Mary Kinsley>I'm unaware of strategic default from the residential sector happening on this scale in American history.
<v Mary Kinsley>据我所知，美国历史上从未发生过这种规模的住宅部门战略性违约。

00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:15.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is Mary Kinsley, a lawyer in Arizona. And Mary talked to our Planet Money colleague and co-toxic asset investor, Alex Blumberg.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是玛丽·金斯利（Mary Kinsley），亚利桑那州的一名律师。玛丽和我们的《金钱星球》同事兼不良资产共同投资者亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）进行了交谈。

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:29.070
<v Alex Blumberg>Mary's job is to answer phones at a help line for people with real estate questions. She says just two years ago, people would call up in tears facing foreclosure, agonizing about how to save their homes. Then, just one year later, everything changed.
<v Alex Blumberg>玛丽的工作是在房地产问题热线电话上接听电话。她说就在两年前，人们还会哭着打电话来面对止赎，痛苦地想着如何保住他们的房子。然后，仅仅一年之后，一切都变了。

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:53.000
<v Mary Kinsley>Now, it's almost a reverse. I'm speaking to people, for the most part, who want to know, "How can I make the bank foreclose on this faster?" And I talk to quite a few people who say, "Hm, perfect credit score versus $80,000 in my pocket and not upside down on this house anymore." It's really not-- it's a business decision, rather than emotional decisions.
<v Mary Kinsley>现在，情况几乎完全反过来了。我在交谈的人，绝大多数都想知道，“我怎样才能让银行更快地对这套房子进行止赎？”我遇到过不少人说，“嗯，完美的信用评分与我口袋里的8万美元相比，而且这套房子不再资不抵债了。”这真的不是——这是一个商业决定，而不是情感决定。

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:56.490
<v Alex Blumberg>And these are people who could continue to pay their mortgage?
<v Alex Blumberg>这些都是有能力继续支付房贷的人吗？

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:12.830
<v Mary Kinsley>Yes. It's not illegal to breach a contract. And that's essentially what it is, it's a breach of contract. And one of the dirty little secrets of real estate is businesses do it all the time.
<v Mary Kinsley>是的。违约并非违法。这本质上就是这样，这是一次违约。房地产行业见不得光的秘密之一就是企业一直在这样做。

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:27.800
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Strategic defaults are going to happen anytime you have plunging home values. But they were particularly bad because of something else that was going on-- another feature of the housing crisis that also shows up in our toxic asset-- speculation.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>每当房价暴跌时，战略性违约就会发生。但它们之所以特别严重，是因为正在发生的另一件事——房地产危机的另一个特征，也出现在我们的不良资产中——投机。

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:30.370
<v Michael Braga>This house is in your toxic asset.
<v Michael Braga>这栋房子在你们的不良资产里。

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:31.900
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is one of ours?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是我们的房子之一？

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.770
<v Michael Braga>Yeah, this is one of yours, that's your house.
<v Michael Braga>对，这是你们的房子之一，那是你的房子。

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:54.390
<v David Kestenbaum>This is Michael Braga, the investigative reporter who's been helping us. This house we're looking at was bought in 2005, at the height of the bubble. But it seems like no one ever moved in, and unlike Richard's condo, it looks like no one ever intended to. Today there are dead palm trees in the front yard with the tops cut off, just eight feet of trunk sticking out of the ground. But the house has promise.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是迈克尔·布拉加，一直协助我们的调查记者。我们正在看的这栋房子购买于2005年泡沫顶峰时期。但似乎从来没有人搬进去过，而且不像理查德的公寓，这里看起来似乎从来没有人打算住进去。今天，前院有死去的棕榈树，树顶被砍掉了，只有八英尺高的树干戳在地上。但这栋房子很有潜力。

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.200
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Oh wow, they have an ocean view?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>噢，哇，他们能看到海景？

00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:05.410
<v Michael Braga>Yeah, that's Sarasota Bay. And the pool is a little green, which means that they haven't put chlorine in it. And all the blinds are down.
<v Michael Braga>对，那是萨拉索塔湾。泳池有点绿，这意味着他们没有往里面加氯。而且所有的百叶窗都拉下来了。

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:07.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I can peek inside.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我可以往里面偷看一下。

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:19.620
<v Michael Braga>Inside, there's some nice white couches, and a bar that looks into the kitchen, oh, and it looks like a Brady Bunch stair case that leads up to the second floor.
<v Michael Braga>里面有一些漂亮的白色沙发，一个能看到厨房的吧台，哦，看起来还有一个像《布雷迪家族》里那种通往二楼的楼梯。

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:33.990
<v David Kestenbaum>This house was bought not as a dream home, not even as a second home-- it was bought in 2005 by a man named Derek Taaca. Braga tells us Taaca is a local lawyer, a purple belt Jiu Jitsu martial artist, and a real estate speculator.
<v David Kestenbaum>这栋房子买来不是为了当梦想之家的，甚至不是作为第二居所——它是在2005年由一个名叫德里克·塔卡（Derek Taaca）的男人买下的。布拉加告诉我们，塔卡是一名当地律师，拥有巴西柔术紫带，还是一名房地产投机商。

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:44.850
<v Michael Braga>Derek Taaca ultimately defaulted on five loans, totaling $3.6 million, including the $991,000 loan that is in your toxic asset.
<v Michael Braga>德里克·塔卡最终在五笔贷款上违约，总额达360万美元，其中包括在你那份不良资产中的99万1千美元贷款。

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:54.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Taaca did not return our phone calls. But his story is not unusual. Of the nine people we tracked down in Florida, three were investors like Taaca-- they were speculators.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>塔卡没有回复我们的电话。但他的故事并不罕见。在我们追踪到的九个佛罗里达人中，有三人像塔卡一样是投资者——他们是投机商。

00:24:54.570 --> 00:25:09.040
<v David Kestenbaum>And it turns out, three out of nine was typical all over the country. According to one estimate, a third of homes bought during the bubble were investments. Waitresses, cab drivers, executives all bought homes, not to live in, but to make them money.
<v David Kestenbaum>事实证明，全国各地九分之三的比例都很典型。据估计，泡沫时期购买的房屋中，有三分之一是投资。女服务员、出租车司机、高管都买房，不是为了住，而是为了赚钱。

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:20.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In Florida, you can see it all over. Homes built, dry wall hung, air conditioners installed, and no one ever moved in. They were empty in 2005, and they're still empty now.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在佛罗里达，你到处都能看到这种景象。建好的房子，挂好的石膏板，装好的空调，却从来没有人搬进去过。它们在2005年是空的，现在依然是空的。

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:41.570
<v David Kestenbaum>So if one third were speculators, that still leaves 2/3. 2/3 of the people in our toxic asset probably bought their homes to live in. Some are still making their payments, some are going through defaults-- not strategic defaults, they don't have a choice.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以如果三分之一是投机商，那还剩下三分之二。我们不良资产中三分之二的人买房可能是为了居住。有些人仍在按时还款，有些人正在经历违约——不是战略性违约，他们别无选择。

00:25:41.570 --> 00:26:07.350
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Flipper: Not the Dolphin. When David and Chana went to Florida to find some of the home owners in their toxic asset, they were not expecting speculators, or an 81 year old with a new set of morals. And they certainly didn't expect to walk into a crime scene, which is what happened next. I should just say that, if you've heard a little bit about mortgage fraud in this crisis, this next story will finally explain exactly what mortgage fraud means. Here's David and Chana.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，炒房客：不是那只海豚。当大卫和查娜去佛罗里达寻找他们不良资产中的一些房主时，他们没有料到会有投机商，也没有料到会遇到一个带有新道德观念的81岁老人。他们当然也没有料到会走进一个犯罪现场，而这正是接下来发生的事。我应该说的是，如果你对这场危机中的抵押贷款欺诈有所耳闻，接下来的这个故事将最终确切地解释什么是抵押贷款欺诈。请听大卫和查娜的讲述。

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:31.220
<v David Kestenbaum>So I'm sitting at my desk, right around the time of our trip to Florida, looking through some court documents, you know, just doing some reporting for our trip. And there's this affidavit filed by an FBI agent listing a handful of mortgages that are apparently fraudulent. There's 202 Island Circle, 1628 Bay Winds Lane-- I'm sort of half paying attention. And then I see this one address-- it's George and Sylvia Bobka. Remember them?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以当时我正坐在办公桌前，大约在我们去佛罗里达的那段日子里，翻阅着一些法庭文件，你知道，只是在为我们的行程做一些采访报道。这里有一份由联邦调查局特工提交的宣誓书，列出了几笔显然具有欺诈性的抵押贷款。有岛屿圆环202号，海风巷1628号——我有点心不在焉。然后我看到了这个地址——是乔治和西尔维娅·博布卡（Sylvia Bobka）。还记得他们吗？

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:38.010
<v Sylvia>Hi, you've reached Sylvia and George, we can't get to the phone right now, but leave a message and we'll get back to you. Thank you.
<v Sylvia>嗨，您联系的是西尔维娅和乔治，我们现在无法接听电话，请留言，我们会给您回电。谢谢。

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:42.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I remember you sent me a heavily exclamation pointed text when that happened, David.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我记得当那件事发生时，你给我发了一条带有大量感叹号的短信，大卫。

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:55.280
<v David Kestenbaum>It was so surprising. I mean there are thousands and thousands of homes in the county, a handful end up listed in this affidavit, and one of them is in our Toxie? We got the court documents from Michael Braga at the Sarasota Herald Tribune .
<v David Kestenbaum>太令人惊讶了。我的意思是，该县有成千上万的房屋，少数几栋最终被列入这份宣誓书，其中一栋居然在我们的托克茜里面？我们从《萨拉索塔先驱论坛报》的迈克尔·布拉加那里得到了法庭文件。

00:26:55.280 --> 00:27:12.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And it turns out, our Toxie is linked to what could be a $100 million mortgage fraud scheme they've been reporting on. Michael told us the whole thing was masterminded by one guy, a guy the folks at the Herald Tribune called the king of the flip.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>事实证明，我们的托克茜与他们一直在报道的一项可能高达1亿美元的抵押贷款欺诈计划有关。迈克尔告诉我们，整起事件是一人一手策划的，那个家伙被《先驱论坛报》的人称为“炒房之王”。

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:38.540
<v Michael Braga>He just may be the biggest case of mortgage fraud in US history. And his name is Craig Adams. And he engineered more than 80 deals, which he has told the FBI about, that were in some way fraudulent. Those 80 deals, properties, were worth about $200 million.
<v Michael Braga>他很可能是美国历史上涉案金额最大的按揭贷款诈骗犯。他的名字叫克雷格·亚当斯。他策划了80多起他已向联邦调查局坦白的交易，这些交易在某种程度上都存在欺诈行为。那80起交易的房产价值大约为2亿美元。

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:48.030
<v David Kestenbaum>So Chana, before when I'd heard about mortgage fraud, I always thought of some couple fudging something on their mortgage application, but this is much, much larger.
<v David Kestenbaum>查娜，以前我听说按揭贷款诈骗时，总以为只是某对夫妻在贷款申请上动了什么手脚，但这起案件的规模要大得多、大得多。

00:27:48.030 --> 00:28:13.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No, in Florida, mortgage fraud appears to have been pretty widespread and organized. The reporters at the Sarasota Herald Tribune have been looking into this one circle, led by Craig Adams, the king of the flip. And it was a kind of fraud scheme based on flipping houses back and forth for higher and higher prices. So people taking out larger and larger loans from the bank, and dividing that money up amongst themselves, among the people in the circle.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不，在佛罗里达州，按揭贷款诈骗似乎相当普遍且有组织。萨拉索塔先驱论坛报的记者一直在调查一个以“转手之王”克雷格·亚当斯为首的圈子。这是一种基于反复倒卖房屋以推高价格的诈骗计划。人们从银行获得越来越大的贷款，并将这笔钱在彼此之间、在圈内人之间瓜分。

00:28:13.400 --> 00:28:27.430
<v David Kestenbaum>We're going to look at how this apparently worked by following one house, this is a house on Cove Terrace in Sarasota, Florida. This one is not in our toxic asset, but George and Sylvia Bobka's house is connected to the same scheme, and their house is right down the street.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们将通过追踪一栋房子来看看这一切是如何运作的，这是佛罗里达州萨拉索塔湾景露台的一栋房子。这栋房子不在我们的不良资产中，但乔治和西尔维娅·博布卡的房子与同一个诈骗计划有关，他们的房子就在这条街上。

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:38.780
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So the story of what happened at Cove Terrace begins with a man who may be the very last person to have truly loved this house. The last percent to own it before it was sold into the circle of flippers.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>湾景露台发生的事情，要从一个可能是最后一个真正热爱这栋房子的人说起。在它被卖给房屋倒卖圈子之前，它是属于最后一个人的。

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:40.380
<v Fred Bloom>Fred Bloom is my name.
<v Fred Bloom>我叫弗雷德·布卢姆。

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:41.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Fred Bloom?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>弗雷德·布卢姆？

