<v Fred Bloom>Fred Bloom. Isn't Dr. Bloom a perfect name for an allergist?
<v Fred Bloom>弗雷德·布卢姆。对过敏症医师来说，“布卢姆”博士（译注：Bloom意为花开）难道不是一个完美的名字吗？

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:47.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Are you an allergist?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>您是一位过敏症医师吗？

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:48.010
<v Fred Bloom>I am.
<v Fred Bloom>我是。

00:28:48.010 --> 00:29:03.670
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Dr. Bloom, the allergist, raised his two daughters in this house. And it's a really nice place, it's on a quiet street, on a cul-de-sac, it's got four bedrooms, red tile roof, it's painted sort of like, you know that Florida, salmoney kind of color? And it has a pool and a boat dock.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>过敏症医师布卢姆博士在这栋房子里养大了他的两个女儿。这是一个非常好的地方，位于一条安静的街道上，在一个死胡同里，有四间卧室，红色的瓦屋顶，油漆的颜色有点像——你知道佛罗里达那种三文鱼的颜色吗？它有一个游泳池和一个船坞。

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:27.630
<v Fred Bloom>We had a little boat, and my kids enjoyed water skiing, and fishing, and family activities. At the time I owned it, it was a two story family room, with windows up above, and you could sit there and watch lightning, and you could watch the sky. Just a warm, warm feeling room, with the big windows and light. So they were good years. You can hear them sort of winding up now.
<v Fred Bloom>我们有一艘小船，我的孩子们喜欢滑水、钓鱼和家庭活动。我拥有它的时候，它是一个两层楼高的家庭活动室，上面有窗户，你可以坐在那里看闪电，看天空。这是一个充满温暖感觉的房间，有大窗户和充足的光线。所以那些都是美好的岁月。你现在能听出它们有点像是在收尾吗？

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:29.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's right, because the kids grow up, huh?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>没错，因为孩子们长大了，对吧？

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:33.900
<v Fred Bloom>Exactly. It was a good time in our lives.
<v Fred Bloom>没错。那是我们生活中美好的时光。

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:45.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And there, appropriately, are the cicadas-- or whatever they are-- right as we get to the drama. The drama began the moment Dr. Bloom decided to sell this house that he had loved.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>恰如其分地，蝉鸣声——或者不管它们是什么——就在我们进入戏剧性情节时响起了。戏剧性的情节始于布卢姆博士决定卖掉这栋他一直深爱的房子的那一刻。

00:29:45.710 --> 00:30:08.580
<v Fred Bloom>It occurred after my wife and I got divorced, and she moved out of town, and I stayed in the house trying to sell it, because she had half equity in the house. And it didn't sell, and it didn't sell, and it didn't sell. My wife wanted to get another Realtor, and we did, and she had the house for a three month contract. And then towards the end of her contract, she became very intent on trying to lower the price so we could sell it.
<v Fred Bloom>这件事发生在我前妻和我离婚之后，她搬出了城，而我留在房子里试图把它卖掉，因为她拥有房子的一半权益。结果房子卖不掉，卖不掉，还是卖不掉。我前妻想换个房地产经纪人，我们照办了，她和经纪人签了三个月的合同。然后在合同快到期时，她变得非常急于降价，这样我们就能把它卖掉。

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:09.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What'd you think about that?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你对此有什么看法？

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:20.970
<v Fred Bloom>I wasn't happy about lowering the price, but my former wife was saying, I want my money, and the Realtor was saying, let's get it sold. And I wasn't happy about that, I was not happy about lowering the price.
<v Fred Bloom>我对降价并不高兴，但我前妻说，我想要我的钱，经纪人则说，赶紧把它卖掉吧。我对这很不高兴，我对降价很不高兴。

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.530
<v Candy Swick>And then we had an offer from Craig Adams.
<v Candy Swick>然后我们收到了克雷格·亚当斯的出价。

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is the Realtor, Candy Swick.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这位是房地产经纪人坎迪·斯威克。

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:41.000
<v David Kestenbaum>Now, we should just point out here that this was the year 2000. Craig Adams was not known at this time as the king of the flip, or as the alleged ring leader of the largest mortgage fraud scheme in Florida history. People called him something else. They called him real estate genius.
<v David Kestenbaum>现在，我们必须在这里指出，那是公元2000年。当时的克雷格·亚当斯还不被称为“转手之王”，也不被认为是佛罗里达州历史上最大按揭贷款诈骗案的疑似头目。人们称呼他别的名字。他们称他为房地产天才。

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:54.870
<v Candy Swick>Craig's offer was very good. It was right on the mark, and when he made the offer it was a fair price. It was like, this is good, take it and run. It's been enough time. This is a very good offer.
<v Candy Swick>克雷格的出价非常好。恰到好处，当他提出报价时，这是一个公平的价格。当时的感觉是，这很好，接受它然后见好就收吧。时间已经够长了。这是一个非常好的报价。

00:30:54.870 --> 00:31:05.560
<v Fred Bloom>And I resisted as long as I could, and then decided the fight is over, so I caved in.
<v Fred Bloom>我尽我所能进行了抵制，然后决定争斗已经结束，所以我妥协了。

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:21.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Dr. Bloom sold the house for $600,000, much less than he wanted for it. And then a few weeks later, he's at the grocery store buying groceries, and he runs into his former Cove Terrace neighbors, and he asks them, "Hey, how you doing? How are the new people on the block?"
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>布卢姆博士以60万美元的价格卖掉了这栋房子，远低于他想要的价钱。然后几周后，他在杂货店买菜，遇到了他在湾景露台的前邻居，他问他们：“嘿，你们过得怎么样？街区里的新住户怎么样？”

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:32.990
<v Fred Bloom>And they told me that my house had sold again, and he mentioned the price of $725,000. And I was really upset. I said, that's $125,000 more than I got for it, and that was only a matter of a couple of weeks.
<v Fred Bloom>他们告诉我，我的房子又卖出去了，他还提到了72.5万美元的价格。我真的非常沮丧。我说，这比我卖掉它时的价格多出了12.5万美元，而且这仅仅是几周内的事。

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:34.240
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Oh, it was weeks?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>哦，只是几周？

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:37.210
<v Fred Bloom>It was just a matter of a couple of weeks. I was not happy.
<v Fred Bloom>仅仅是几周的时间。我很不高兴。

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:55.840
<v David Kestenbaum>Dr. Bloom had no idea what was going on, and he didn't for 10 years, until he gets a phone call from one of the reporters at the Sarasota Herald Tribune . So the Sarasota Herald Tribune was looking into what seemed like a lot of illegal flipping around Florida, and Craig Adams's name came up several times including, on the Cove Terrace house.
<v David Kestenbaum>布卢姆博士根本不知道发生了什么，而且在长达10年的时间里一直被蒙在鼓里，直到他接到萨拉索塔先驱论坛报的一位记者的电话。当时萨拉索塔先驱论坛报正在调查佛罗里达州各地看似非法的房屋倒卖活动，克雷格·亚当斯的名字多次出现，其中就包括湾景露台的这栋房子。

00:31:55.840 --> 00:32:24.960
<v David Kestenbaum>So one of the reporters, Matthew Doig, calls up Dr. Bloom, and he says, "Do you remember the details of selling that house?" Thinking Bloom probably won't remember anything, it's been 10 years. But Bloom remembered every detail of the sale, he had held onto this resentment and to this mystery for 10 years. Why did he try to sell, for over a year, try to sell his house for $700,000, and eventually have to sell it for $600,000, and then weeks later, this guy Craig Adams turns around and sells it for $725,000. Here's that reporter, Matthew Doig.
<v David Kestenbaum>于是，其中一位记者马修·多伊格给布卢姆博士打电话，他说：“你还记得卖那栋房子的细节吗？”他以为布卢姆可能什么都不记得了，毕竟已经过去了10年。但布卢姆记得销售的每一个细节，这10年来他一直怀恨在心，并对这个谜团耿耿于怀。为什么他试图卖房，一年多来试图以70万美元的价格卖掉他的房子，最终却不得不以60万美元的价格出售，而在几周后，这个名叫克雷格·亚当斯的家伙转手就以72.5万美元的价格把它卖掉了。这位记者就是马修·多伊格。

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:38.250
<v Matthew Doig>After Dr. Bloom is out of the picture, that house is completely controlled by Craig Adams, and every time it is sold, Adams is representing both the buyer and the seller. So he's completely controlling the process.
<v Matthew Doig>在布卢姆博士退出之后，这栋房子就完全由克雷格·亚当斯掌控了，每次它被出售时，亚当斯都同时代表买方和卖方。所以他完全掌控了整个流程。

00:32:38.250 --> 00:33:10.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And that house sold many, many times. And in fact, to Dr. Bloom, it looked like he sold the house to Craig Adams, but Craig Adams actually got his friend Steve Wexler to sign for it. So Steve Wexler buys it from Dr. Bloom, and then Steve Wexler sells it to John Keyworth for $725,000, who sells it to Craig Adams-- who actually buys it at one point-- for $1.1 million, who sells it to Kelly West for almost one and a half million, who sells it again to Charles Scott Abel for close to $2 million.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而那栋房子被卖掉了非常多次。事实上，对布卢姆博士来说，看起来他是把房子卖给了克雷格·亚当斯，但克雷格·亚当斯实际上是让他的朋友史蒂夫·韦克斯勒代为签字。于是史蒂夫·韦克斯勒从布卢姆博士手中买下了它，然后史蒂夫·韦克斯勒以72.5万美元的价格把它卖给约翰·基沃斯，约翰·基沃斯又以110万美元的价格把它卖给克雷格·亚当斯——克雷格·亚当斯在某个时候确实买下了它——接着亚当斯又以近150万美元的价格把它卖给凯利·韦斯特，凯利·韦斯特又以接近200万美元的价格把它卖给查尔斯·斯科特·阿贝尔。

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:14.380
<v David Kestenbaum>So it sells five times in four years, and basically triples in price.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以它在四年内卖了五次，价格基本翻了三倍。

00:33:14.380 --> 00:33:25.200
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, and Dr. Bloom the whole time is really perplexed about what is happening. He still lives in the neighborhood, and the house keeps going up in price, but it doesn't look better. It kind of looks worse.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的，而布卢姆博士在这期间对发生的事情感到非常困惑。他仍然住在那个社区里，房子的价格一直在上涨，但它看起来并没有变好。它看起来甚至更糟了。

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:31.650
<v Fred Bloom>Occasionally I would drive by the house and drive through the neighborhood, and at various times the yard was really in disrepair.
<v Fred Bloom>我偶尔会开车经过这栋房子，开车穿过这个社区，在不同的时间里，院子里真的失于打理。

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:34.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When it was in disrepair, did it look like no one was living here?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当它失于打理时，看起来像没有人住在这里吗？

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:36.730
<v Fred Bloom>Yes. It looked like it was vacant.
<v Fred Bloom>是的。看起来像是空置的。

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:57.620
<v David Kestenbaum>So the way the Tribune lays this out, Keyworth, Wexler, all these guys were connected to Adams. They were part of his circle. The idea here is that with each sale, the new owner would take out a mortgage loan and use that to pay the previous owner for the house. The previous owner has now made money, because he sold it for more than he bought it for. And that profit, allegedly, gets split among the players.
<v David Kestenbaum>根据论坛报披露的情况，基沃斯、韦克斯勒，所有这些人都与亚当斯有关。他们是他圈子里的成员。这里的思路是，每次交易时，新业主都会获得一笔按揭贷款，并用这笔贷款向房子的前业主支付房款。前业主现在赚钱了，因为他卖出的价格比他买入时的价格高。据说这笔利润会在各方之间进行瓜分。

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:19.120
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So I borrow $600,000 from the bank to buy the house, and then a year later, I sell it to my buddy Keyworth for $800,000. Keyworth gets a loan from the bank, $800,000. And since we're working together, we now have just made $200,000 to split between us. That's the allegation anyway, it's still working its way through the legal system.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我向银行借了60万美元买下这栋房子，然后一年后，我以80万美元的价格把它卖给我的好友基沃斯。基沃斯从银行获得了一笔80万美元的贷款。既然我们在合作，我们现在就赚到了20万美元可以在我们之间瓜分。无论如何，这就是指控的内容，它仍在通过法律系统进行审理。

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:30.790
<v David Kestenbaum>When the housing bubble popped in 2007, the Cove Terrace house was owned by yet another associate of Craig Adams's, and he defaults on more than $2 million in loans. The bank forecloses on the house.
<v David Kestenbaum>当2007年住房泡沫破裂时，湾景露台的房子由克雷格·亚当斯的另一个同伙拥有，而他未能偿还超过200万美元的贷款。银行对这栋房子进行了法拍。

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:44.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And this is when Candy Swick, Dr. Bloom's real estate agent, finally figures out something funny has been going on. She has another client interested in the Cove Terrace house, and for the second time, 10 years later, she pulls up the listing.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就在这时，布卢姆博士的房地产经纪人坎迪·斯威克终于发现有些不对劲了。她有另一个客户对湾景露台的房子感兴趣，于是10年后的她第二次调出了房源信息。

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:45.270
<v Candy Swick>Oh my gosh.
<v Candy Swick>哦，天哪。

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:46.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What did it look like?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它看起来是什么样的？

00:34:46.030 --> 00:34:59.860
<v Candy Swick>It looked like it had been sold every couple of years. And I see, Craig Adams, listed and sold. Craig Adams, listed and sold. Craig Adams, listed and sold. Craig Adams, listed and sold. And it's like, this is not right.
<v Candy Swick>它看起来就像每隔几年就被卖掉一次。我看到，克雷格·亚当斯，挂牌并售出。克雷格·亚当斯，挂牌并售出。克雷格·亚当斯，挂牌并售出。克雷格·亚当斯，挂牌并售出。当时的感觉就是，这太不对劲了。

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:17.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Not right and isn't it also stupid? This is the thing that always confused me about this kind of fraud. Because at the end of the day, all you've done is inflate the value of a house and take out larger and larger loans. And at the end, you've just got this bigger loan than you started with. You've still got to repay that. What the hell were the people in this circle thinking?
<v David Kestenbaum>不仅不对劲，而且不是很愚蠢吗？这就是我一直对这种欺诈行为感到困惑的地方。因为到头来，你所做的一切不过是抬高了房子的价值，并获得了越来越大的贷款。最后，你手里的贷款比一开始时还要多。你仍然必须偿还这笔贷款。这个圈子里的人到底脑子里在想什么？

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:38.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well, in some ways, it could be the perfect crime. The last guy defaults on the house, as he did with Craig Adams's house, and that guy's credit is ruined, but he and everyone else gets to keep the money from the sales. And it's a lot of money. Though Guy Cecala, publisher of Inside Mortgage Finance , says he doesn't think these guys were that methodical.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯，在某些方面，这可能是一场完美的犯罪。最后一个人对这栋房子断供了，就像克雷格·亚当斯房子的情形一样，那家伙的信用毁了，但他和其他所有人都得以保留了销售所得的资金。这是一大笔钱。尽管《内幕按揭金融》杂志的出版商盖伊·塞卡拉表示，他认为这些人并没有那么有条理。

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:55.060
<v Guy Cecala>There wasn't a great exit strategy. In hindsight, these weren't geniuses figuring out these plans, because they had a lot of pitfalls. Most of them saw quick money right away and weren't figuring out how it was going to play out over time.
<v Guy Cecala>没有一个很好的退出策略。事后看来，这些人并不是什么想出这些计划的天才，因为其中有很多隐患。他们中的大多数人立刻看到了快钱，却没有去想它随着时间推移会如何发展。

00:35:55.060 --> 00:36:13.640
<v David Kestenbaum>Craig Adams, the king of the flip, is now an FBI informant, helping the feds investigate the circle, according to the guys at the Herald Tribune . The closing agent, who did a lot of the paper work for Craig Adams's deals on this, has been arrested. Adams did not return our calls.
<v David Kestenbaum>据先驱论坛报的记者透露，“转手之王”克雷格·亚当斯现在是一名联邦调查局线人，正在协助联邦调查局调查这个圈子。为克雷格·亚当斯的这些交易办理了大量文书工作的过户代理人已经被捕。亚当斯没有回我们的电话。

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:21.340
<v David Kestenbaum>[MUSIC - "THE TOXIE SONG" BY JAMES GAMMON]
<v David Kestenbaum>[音乐 - 詹姆斯·加蒙演唱的《托克西之歌》]

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:28.760
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So, David, I think we can say that our dream that Toxie would be an encyclopedia of the financial crisis, that part's working out.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以，大卫，我想我们可以说，我们关于让“托克西”成为金融危机百科全书的梦想，这一部分正在实现。

00:36:28.760 --> 00:36:38.680
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, she's got a retired guy walking away from his home, speculators, possibly fraud, and of course, some investors who bought the toxic asset without really knowing what the hell they were doing.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，她拥有一个放弃自己房屋的退休老人、投机者、可能的欺诈行为，当然，还有一些在根本不知道自己在搞什么鬼的情况下买下这笔不良资产的投资者。

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:55.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们。

00:36:55.330 --> 00:37:24.450
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, why carpenters in New Jersey hate Toxie. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues. It's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, Toxie. We try to understand the entire economic crisis through the story of a single toxic asset. The reporters on our story today are Planet Money's David Kestenbaum and Chana Joffe-Walt. We've arrived at act four of our show.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，为什么新泽西州的木匠讨厌托克西。稍后继续，由芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台为您带来我们的节目。这里是美国生活，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种故事。今天的节目是：托克西。我们试图通过一个单一不良资产的故事来理解整场经济危机。今天我们节目的记者是“金钱星球”的大卫·克斯滕鲍姆和查娜·约夫-沃尔特。我们已经来到了节目的第四幕。

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:53.890
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Villains and How to Sue Them. January, February, these were happy months for Toxie, the toxic asset. Our team bought it in January for a thousand. In February, just like they hoped, first payment came in, a check for $141. And then, things started to take a turn. March's check was a lot less-- $44.81. Toxie was getting sick, quickly. So David and Chana called Wit Solberg in Kansas City, the guy who helped them buy Toxie in the first place.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，恶棍与如何起诉他们。一月、二月，对不良资产托克西来说是快乐的几个月。我们的团队在一月份以一千美元买下了它。二月份，正如他们所希望的那样，第一笔付款到账了，一张141美元的支票。然后，情况开始发生转变。三月的支票少了很多——44.81美元。托克西病得很快。于是大卫和查娜打电话给堪萨斯城的维特·索尔伯格，也就是最初帮他们买下托克西的那个人。

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:55.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We have a lot of questions for you.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们有很多问题想问你。

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:59.160
<v Wit Solber>Yeah. I imagine you do.
<v Wit Solber>嗯。我想你们确实有。

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:05.340
<v Ira Glass>Questions with no clear answer. Meanwhile, other things were happening. Chana got an email. Looked urgent.
<v Ira Glass>没有明确答案的问题。与此同时，其他事情正在发生。查娜收到了一封电子邮件。看起来很紧急。

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:25.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Subject, do you know your toxic asset is being sued. Immediate thought, what the hell does that mean? In a moment of confusion, I thought, wait, are we being sued? In the end, it turns out the lawsuit is against the creators of our toxic asset. And the man doing the suing is as close as we could ever get to Toxie's previous owner.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>主题：你知道你的不良资产正面临诉讼吗。第一反应是，这到底是什么意思？在一片混乱中，我想，等等，是我们正在被诉讼吗？最后，事实证明，诉讼对象是我们的不良资产的创造者。而提起诉讼的人，是我们所能找到的、最接近托克西前业主的人。

00:38:25.750 --> 00:38:49.080
<v David Kestenbaum>When we bought Toxie in Kansas City, we had no idea who we were buying her from. We pictured a bank, we pictured a Wall Street guy with a nice car driving down the West Side Highway. And we kept searching for him. We called out for him on national radio, on television. If you owned this bond, please get in touch with us. We didn't hear anything. We put an ad in what we imagined was his favorite publication, a newsletter called Asset Backed Alert, nothing.
<v David Kestenbaum>当我们在堪萨斯城买下托克西时，我们根本不知道我们是从谁手里买下她的。我们想象着是一家银行，我们想象着是一个开着豪车在西区公路飞驰的华尔街大亨。我们一直在寻找他。我们在全国广播电台、电视上呼唤他。如果你拥有这只债券，请与我们联系。我们没有听到任何消息。我们在我们想象中他最喜欢的刊物——一份名为《资产支持警报》的简报上登了广告，什么也没有。

00:38:49.080 --> 00:39:02.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But this law suit, this law suit got us someone close, a toxic asset investor who bought a close sibling of Toxie. His name is George Laufenberg. And meeting him, we realized, maybe we were looking in the wrong circles.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但是这场诉讼，这场诉讼让我们找到了一个接近的人，一个买下托克西“近亲”的不良资产投资者。他的名字叫乔治·劳芬贝格。遇见他后，我们意识到，也许我们找错了圈子。

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:13.570
<v George Laufenberg>I'm actually a carpenter out of Local 623, Atlantic City, and I've been a member of the Carpenters Union since 1972, which puts me at about 38 years.
<v George Laufenberg>其实我是大西洋城623地方工会的木匠，自1972年以来我一直木匠工会的会员，算下来我已经干了大约38年。

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:31.870
<v David Kestenbaum>George is the administrative manager of the New Jersey Carpenters Funds. He manages their pension fund, their health benefits, and their vacation fund. He bought his Toxie as an investment for the vacation fund. And that is the last you're going to hear from George, because George, in his effort to sue Toxie's creators, has hired a lawyer, who prefers to speak for him.
<v David Kestenbaum>乔治是新泽西州木匠基金会的行政经理。他管理着他们的养老金基金、健康福利基金和休假基金。他买下他的托克西作为休假基金的投资。这也是你将从乔治那里听到的最后的话，因为乔治为了起诉托克西的创造者，雇了一位律师，后者更愿意替他发言。

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:55.090
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>George owns what you could call Toxie's younger sister. So Toxie's real name is HVMLT2005, that stands for Harbor View Mortgage Loan Trust 2005, because all the mortgages in ours were from 2005. The carpenters have HVMLT2006, so they bought the 2006 model with 2006 mortgages. Same people put her together just a year later.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>乔治拥有你可以称之为托克西妹妹的东西。所以托克西的真名是HVMLT2005，这代表“2005年海景按揭贷款信托”，因为我们那里的所有按揭贷款都来自2005年。木匠们拥有HVMLT2006，所以他们买的是2006年的型号，里面有2006年的按揭贷款。同样一拨人仅仅一年后就把她拼凑在了一起。

00:39:55.090 --> 00:40:18.570
<v David Kestenbaum>Though as George's lawyer is quick to point out, George bought his Toxie way back when no one knew she was Toxie. He did not, like us, buy his as an experiment. He didn't go to Kansas City to rifle through the bargain basement. He bought what he thought was a pristine investment grade bond and paid full price. The carpenters spent $100,000, and that investment is now worth just $5,000.
<v David Kestenbaum>尽管正如乔治的律师急于指出的那样，乔治买他的托克西时，根本没有人知道她是托克西。他不像我们那样把购买当成一场实验。他没有去堪萨斯城的廉价地下室里淘宝。他买下的是他认为是一笔纯净的投资级债券，并且支付了全价。木匠们花掉了10万美元，而那笔投资现在只值5000美元。

00:40:18.570 --> 00:40:27.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So George the carpenter is furious, and he's trying to do something we've been trying to do-- figure out what's going on inside a single toxic asset.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以木匠乔治怒不可遏，他正在尝试做一件我们一直在尝试做的事情——弄清楚单一不良资产内部到底发生了什么。

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:34.450
<v David Kestenbaum>The only difference is, we have Google and a phone, the carpenters have a team of fancy Manhattan lawyers, including Joel Laitman.
<v David Kestenbaum>唯一的区别是，我们有谷歌和电话，而木匠们拥有一支由曼哈顿顶级律师组成的团队，其中包括乔尔·莱特曼。

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:38.030
<v Joel Laitman>It's a complex chain of people involved.
<v Joel Laitman>这是一条错综复杂的人员参与链。

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:41.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So is it hard sometimes to figure out who to sue?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那么有时候弄清楚该起诉谁是不是很难？

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:45.040
<v Joel Laitman>You just have to read the offering documents very carefully.
<v Joel Laitman>你只需要非常仔细地阅读募股文件。

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:47.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Just like 5,000 pages, right?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就像5000页那样，对吧？

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:49.870
<v Joel Laitman>Yeah. Just read them and read them again.
<v Joel Laitman>对。只需读了又读。

00:40:49.870 --> 00:41:17.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We're sitting in this conference room, and in front of Joel Laitman there is a huge stack of papers, almost up to my collar bone, with colored tabs, big blocked out, highlighted sections, basically reconstructing, in more detail than what we have done, who made this monster? Who is at fault? He's asking the question most of us would love to know an answer to. Who is to blame for these things? Though, as a lawyer, he's asking that with a definite goal in mind-- who can he sue?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们坐在会议室里，乔尔·莱特曼（Joel Laitman）面前堆着厚厚一叠文件，几乎快到我的锁骨那么高，上面贴着彩色的标签，大段大段地用黑笔涂黑、做记号。基本上，他正在比我们做得更细致地重构：是谁造了这个怪物？谁该承担责任？他正在提出我们大多数人都很想知道答案的问题：这些事情到底该怪谁？不过，作为一名律师，他提出这个问题的脑海中有着明确的目标——他能起诉谁？

00:41:17.890 --> 00:41:33.880
<v David Kestenbaum>Well, let's run through the possible suspects. Toxie's life, and the life of her sibling that the carpenters bought, started with a huge subprime mortgage lender, Countrywide. Countrywide gave out all the loans in Toxie, all those loans that are going bad. But Countrywide collapsed. They're not around anymore.
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧，让我们梳理一下可能的嫌疑人。托克西（Toxie）的生活，以及木匠们买下的她那份兄弟资产的生活，始于一家巨大的次级抵押贷款机构——全国金融公司（Countrywide）。全国金融公司发放了托克西里的所有贷款，也就是那些正在变坏的贷款。但全国金融公司倒闭了。他们已经不在了。

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:52.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>OK, so how about the rating agencies? They rated this stuff as very good investments. Maybe you can sue them. The lawyers tried that. The judge dismissed it. Like most cases against rating agencies, the rating agencies argue, "We're just like movie critics or restaurant reviewers, we're expressing an opinion protected by the First Amendment."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好吧，那评级机构呢？他们把这些东西评为非常好的投资。也许你可以起诉他们。律师们试过了。法官驳回了。就像大多数针对评级机构的案件一样，评级机构辩称：“我们就像影评人或餐厅评论家，我们表达的是受第一修正案保护的观点。”

00:41:52.990 --> 00:42:08.360
<v David Kestenbaum>So that leaves the sellers of the toxic asset. In this case, the Royal Bank of Scotland, RBS. They bought mortgages from Countrywide, packaged them into complex securities, and they were the guys who sold them as super safe investments to people like George the carpenter and his pension fund.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以剩下的就是有毒资产的卖家了。在这个案子里，是苏格兰皇家银行（RBS）。他们向全国金融公司购买了抵押贷款，将其打包成复杂的证券，并且正是他们把这些东西作为超级安全的投资卖给了像木匠乔治和他的养老基金这样的人。

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:30.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The problem with that is you can't just say, look, RBS sold me this thing that's now worthless. RBS could always argue, we didn't know it was going to be worthless. No, you have to show-- you have to prove-- they misled you in some way. And so Joel, the carpenters' lawyer, went on a quest for what is the smoking gun of securities lawyerdom-- a misstatement.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里的问题在于，你不能只是说，听着，RBS卖给我的这个东西现在一文不值了。RBS总是可以辩称，我们不知道它会变得一文不值。不，你必须展示——你必须证明——他们在某种程度上误导了你。因此，木匠们的律师乔尔开始寻找证券律师界的“冒烟的枪”——虚假陈述。

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:38.070
<v David Kestenbaum>A misstatement. Not a bunch of secret emails conspiring against the carpenters, not a clandestine recording of an RBS banker plot.
<v David Kestenbaum>虚假陈述。不是一堆密谋对抗木匠的秘密电子邮件，不是RBS银行家阴谋的秘密录音。

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:48.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No, a tiny technical phrase, maybe a couple, buried deep in documents that came with the toxic asset that the lawyers can prove would mislead an investor.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不，是一个微小的技术性短语，也许是一两个，埋藏在有毒资产附带的文件深处，律师们可以证明这些短语会误导投资者。

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:53.920
<v Joel Laitman>To find a misstatement or omission, you have to read the whole thing, many times.
<v Joel Laitman>要找到虚假陈述或遗漏，你必须把整份文件读很多遍。

00:42:53.920 --> 00:42:55.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Where is that, do you actually have that tagged?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在哪里，你真的把那个做上标记了吗？

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:58.040
<v Joel Laitman>Yeah, sure.
<v Joel Laitman>对，当然。

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So here we're on page 81.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以现在我们翻到了第81页。

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:07.410
<v Joel Laitman>It starts at 80-- this is mortgage loan origination is the section, Countrywide. All of this.
<v Joel Laitman>它从第80页开始——这一节是抵押贷款发放，全国金融公司。所有这些。

00:43:07.410 --> 00:43:10.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And you're underlining over here.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你在旁边画线了。

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:14.820
<v Joel Laitman>Yeah, very exciting. It actually was exciting.
<v Joel Laitman>对，非常令人兴奋。其实确实很兴奋。

00:43:14.820 --> 00:43:38.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Page 81 of the offering document put out by RBS when they sold the toxic asset, Laitman found what he thinks is his misstatement. Three lines stating that the underlying collateral in the mortgage backed securities had underwriting guidelines that required a credit check-- a credit check that does not seem to have been performed on all of these loans. Ladies and gentlemen, we have a case.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在RBS出售有毒资产时发布的募股文件的第81页上，莱特曼找到了他认为属于虚假陈述的内容。有三行文字指出，抵押贷款支持证券中的底层资产有要求进行信用检查的承销准则——而这项信用检查似乎并没有在所有这些贷款中执行。女士们先生们，我们有案子可打了。

00:43:38.610 --> 00:44:00.480
<v David Kestenbaum>There are offices of lawyers throughout the country searching for their page 81 right now. Because it's not just carpenters in New Jersey who want their money back. PIMCO, which manages the world's largest mutual fund, does too. So do Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, those huge government sponsored mortgage lenders that ended up with toxic assets. And the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. The Fed, by the way, owns another of Toxie's close siblings.
<v David Kestenbaum>全国各地的律师事务所现在都在寻找属于他们的第81页。因为不仅仅是新泽西州的木匠想要回他们的钱。管理着世界最大共同基金的品浩（PIMCO）也想要。房利美和房地美也是如此，那些最终持有有毒资产的庞大政府资助抵押贷款机构。还有纽约联邦储备银行。顺便说一句，美联储还拥有托克西的另一个近亲资产。

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:12.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Investors all over the globe want their money back, potentially, $200 billion they believe they are owed, because somewhere, in some document, someone misrepresented what they were selling. These investors lost a lot of money, and they want someone to pay.
<v David Kestenbaum>全球各地的投资者都想要回他们的钱，潜在金额高达2000亿美元，他们认为这是他们应得的，因为在某个地方、某份文件中，有人歪曲了他们所销售的东西。这些投资者损失了大量的钱，他们想要有人为此买单。

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:15.410
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>If you win, who pays you?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>如果你赢了，谁来付钱给你？

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:18.790
<v Joel Laitman>It'll be the defendants who are in the case, so we're talking about--
<v Joel Laitman>将是案子里的被告，所以我们谈论的是——

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:19.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>RBS?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>RBS?

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:21.000
<v Joel Laitman>RBS.
<v Joel Laitman>RBS.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:38.200
<v David Kestenbaum>And that may sound satisfying, RBS sold these guys a bum investment. Who cares if it's a tiny misstatement in thousands of pages that probably no on ever read? It's like Al Capone getting caught on tax evasion. Justice is still being served, the little guy carpenters are getting justice from the big, rich, international bank.
<v David Kestenbaum>这听起来可能挺令人满意，RBS向这些人卖出了一项糟糕的投资。谁会在乎这是否只是成千上万页中可能没人读过的微小虚假陈述呢？这就像阿尔·卡彭（Al Capone）因逃税落网一样。正义仍然得到了伸张，底层木匠正在从庞大、富有、国际化的银行那里获得正义。

00:44:38.200 --> 00:44:38.950
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但是——

00:44:38.950 --> 00:44:40.060
<v David Kestenbaum>But there's a but.
<v David Kestenbaum>但有个“但是”。

00:44:40.060 --> 00:44:57.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Guess who owns RBS? The British taxpayers. RBS, like other big banks, failed. RBS failed so spectacularly that the British government ended up taking over the bank, investing billions of dollars of taxpayer money to keep RBS afloat.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>猜猜谁拥有RBS？英国纳税人。RBS和其他大银行一样失败了。RBS的失败是如此惨烈，以至于英国政府最终接管了这家银行，投入了数十亿美元的纳税人资金来维持RBS的运营。

00:44:57.370 --> 00:45:05.740
<v David Kestenbaum>Which means, if this suit is successful, it won't be fat cat RBS bankers paying back the carpenters, it'll be regular British taxpayers.
<v David Kestenbaum>这意味着，如果这场诉讼成功，付钱给木匠的将不是那些脑满肠肥的RBS银行家，而是普通的英国纳税人。

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:07.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What pub is this?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是什么酒吧？

00:45:07.720 --> 00:45:14.520
<v Chris Hayes>The Marquis of Westminster, 50 Warwick Way, London, UK.
<v Chris Hayes>威斯敏斯特侯爵酒吧，英国伦敦沃里克路50号。

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:15.760
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You're the manager?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你是经理吗？

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:16.890
<v Chris Hayes>I'm the owner.
<v Chris Hayes>我是老板。

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:18.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Are you a British tax payer?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你是英国纳税人吗？

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:19.820
<v Chris Hayes>I am.
<v Chris Hayes>我是。

00:45:19.820 --> 00:45:23.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And what do you know about the bail out of British banks?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那你对英国银行的救助了解多少？

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:34.380
<v Chris Hayes>What do I know? I know that the banks lost money, and we paid-- I think the Royal Bank of Scotland-- 74% we bought them out. Bought their [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Chris Hayes>我知道什么？我知道银行亏钱了，而我们付了钱——我想苏格兰皇家银行——我们买下了74%的股份。买下了他们的[听不清]。

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:38.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So in some senses, you own RBS?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以在某种意义上，你拥有RBS？

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:40.600
<v Chris Hayes>We do, yeah, the tax payer does.
<v Chris Hayes>是的，纳税人确实拥有。

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:41.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You and the guys in your pub?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你和你的酒吧里的伙计们？

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:43.650
<v Chris Hayes>Yeah.
<v Chris Hayes>对。

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:47.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>How did you feel about it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你对此有什么感觉？

00:45:47.290 --> 00:46:02.530
<v Chris Hayes>You know, I think it's outrageous. Because at the end of the day, these guys risk a hell of a lot, and if that risk pays off, they get paid a hell a lot of money. I think it's disgusting, personally.
<v Chris Hayes>你知道，我认为这太离谱了。因为归根结底，这些家伙冒了极大的风险，如果这种风险得到了回报，他们就会拿到海量的金钱。我个人认为这很令人作呕。

00:46:02.530 --> 00:46:05.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did you feel like it was necessary, though?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不过，你觉得这有必要吗？

00:46:05.710 --> 00:46:11.270
<v Chris Hayes>What, that we bailed them out? No, I'd let them go under. [BLEEP], that's what I say.
<v Chris Hayes>什么，我们救助他们？不，我会让它们倒闭。[粗口]，这就是我的看法。

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:33.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Chris Hayes, the owner of the Marquis of Westminster pub. And it's actually a lot worse than Chris thought. British taxpayers now have an 84% stake in the bank. I told him about the carpenters and how they lost so much money, how they were suing RBS, which, as a British citizen, Chris the pub owner now partially owns. And when I told him that, he got a little more thoughtful.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>克里斯·海耶斯（Chris Hayes），威斯敏斯特侯爵酒吧的老板。而现实其实比克里斯想象的还要糟糕得多。英国纳税人现在拥有该银行84%的股份。我告诉他关于木匠们以及他们损失了多少钱、他们如何起诉RBS的事，作为英国公民，身为酒吧老板的克里斯现在部分拥有这家银行。当我告诉他这些时，他变得更有思索了。

00:46:33.940 --> 00:47:04.240
<v Chris Hayes>When you buy something, I suppose you take the responsibility of what the company's done. So I suppose it's inevitable, really, to a certain degree. We're buying into their mistakes, I suppose. If it's a bunch of average men on the street trying to get their money back, I suppose I wouldn't mind so much. I mean, if American Express or BP or somebody were filing the suit, then I'd be a little bit more reticent in giving the money. But, you know, it's a bunch of carpenters. And good luck to them.
<v Chris Hayes>当你买下某样东西时，我想你要承担这家公司所做事情的责任。所以我想这在某种程度上确实是不可避免的。我想我们是在为他们的错误买单。如果是一群街头普通人想要回他们的钱，我想我不会介意太多。我的意思是，如果美国运通或BP（英国石油）或某家公司提起诉讼，那么我在掏钱时就会更加犹豫一点。但是，你知道的，这是一群木匠。祝他们好运。

00:47:04.240 --> 00:47:05.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You don't mind if your money goes to them?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你不介意你的钱流向他们吗？

00:47:05.640 --> 00:47:28.140
<v Chris Hayes>No, I don't mind. I mean, it's far removed from me, isn't it? If I was asked to actually physically put my hand in my pocket and hand the money over, it'd be different, wouldn't it?
<v Chris Hayes>不，我不介意。我的意思是，这离我很远，不是吗？如果有人要求我真的把手伸进口袋把钱交出来，那感觉就不同了，不是吗？

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:33.440
<v Ira Glass>That brings us to Act Five, Toxie in a Coma. I Know, It's Serious.
<v Ira Glass>这把我们带到了第五幕：陷入昏迷的托克西。我知道，这很严重。

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:48.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>After the initial scare in March, when our monthly check was only $44, Toxie looked like she was recovering. The next check we got in April was for almost double that-- $72. But then, in May, a brutal wake up call from Wit Solberg.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在三月份初次受到惊吓——当时我们的月度支票只有44美元——之后，托克西看起来正在康复。我们在四月份收到的下一张支票几乎是那个金额的两倍——72美元。但随后，在五月份，维特·索尔伯格（Wit Solberg）给我们敲响了一记残酷的警钟。

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:53.690
<v Wit Solberg>Well the patient, Toxie, didn't get a payment this month.
<v Wit Solberg>嗯，病人托克西这个月没有收到付款。

00:47:53.690 --> 00:47:57.430
<v David Kestenbaum>In other words-- so we're supposed to get a check every month, how much is our check for this month?
<v David Kestenbaum>换句话说——所以我们应该每个月都收到一张支票，我们这个月的支票是多少钱？

00:47:57.430 --> 00:47:59.060
<v Wit Solberg>It's for zero.
<v Wit Solberg>是零。

00:47:59.060 --> 00:47:59.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Zero?
<v David Kestenbaum>零？

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:01.930
<v Wit Solberg>Yeah, there wasn't a check.
<v Wit Solberg>对，没有支票。

00:48:01.930 --> 00:48:04.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, wait, nothing? We're not getting anything?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，等等，什么都没有？我们什么也得不到？

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:07.860
<v Wit Solberg>Hey, you're still alive though.
<v Wit Solberg>嘿，不过你还活着。

00:48:07.860 --> 00:48:10.030
<v David Kestenbaum>She's sort of in a coma.
<v David Kestenbaum>她有点像在昏迷中。

00:48:10.030 --> 00:48:11.160
<v Wit Solberg>A dazed state.
<v Wit Solberg>处于恍惚状态。

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:13.850
<v David Kestenbaum>A dazed state.
<v David Kestenbaum>一种恍惚状态。

00:48:13.850 --> 00:48:18.310
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We have made back half of our money, how should we feel right now?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们已经收回了一半的钱，现在我们该有什么感觉？

00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:26.990
<v Wit Solberg>At least you got half. Right? The glass is half full.
<v Wit Solberg>至少你拿回了一半。对吧？杯子是半满的。

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:31.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Seriously? The glass is half full? We got a check for zero this month.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>真的吗？杯子是半满的？我们这个月收到的支票是零。

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:32.120
<v David Kestenbaum>That's the other half of the glass.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是杯子的另一半。

00:48:32.120 --> 00:48:43.880
<v Wit Solberg>That's the other half of the glass. No, that's right.
<v Wit Solberg>那是杯子的另一半。不，没错。

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:56.170
<v David Kestenbaum>This wasn't the way it was supposed to happen. Back in the bond cave Rob had explained to us that we'd get checks for roughly the same amount each month, and then one day they'd stop. They're not supposed to go up and down, and they're definitely not supposed to be for $0.
<v David Kestenbaum>事情本不该是这样的。回到债券洞穴时，罗布向我们解释过，我们每个月都会收到大约相同金额的支票，然后有一天它们会停止。它们本不应该忽高忽低，而且绝对不应该是0美元。

00:48:56.170 --> 00:49:18.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So we spent $1,000. If Toxie stayed in a coma, we'd be out a lot of money. To try to sort out what was going on, we brought in Samir Noriega and Steve Marcus from NPM Capital. Samir used to trade Toxie's ancestors at JP Morgan. And he pulled up Toxie's paperwork and looked at it sort of the way a doctor looks at an x-ray, and pointed.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我们花了1000美元。如果托克西一直昏迷不醒，我们将损失一大笔钱。为了弄清楚发生了什么，我们请来了NPM资本公司的萨米尔·诺里加（Samir Noriega）和史蒂夫·马库斯（Steve Marcus）。萨米尔以前在摩根大通交易托克西的“祖先”资产。他调出了托克西的纸质文件，看着它们，那眼神有点像医生看着X光片，然后指着说：

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:21.510
<v Samir Noriega>Here, I'll show you, real quick.
<v Samir Noriega>来，我很快给你们看一眼。

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:31.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So he finds a loan in Brentwood, California, for $696,000, and he tells us, something big happened to this Brentwood guy this last month.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>于是他在加州布伦特伍德（Brentwood）找到了一笔696,000美元的贷款，并告诉我们，上个月这个布伦特伍德的家伙身上发生了一件大事。

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:37.330
<v Samir Noriega>In this case, here you have $200,000 that were forgiven, of principal.
<v Samir Noriega>在这个案子里，这里有200,000美元的本金被免除了。

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:42.310
<v David Kestenbaum>So you can see there, so they really just said, "Don't worry about that $200,000, you don't have to pay it."
<v David Kestenbaum>所以你可以从那里看到，他们实际上只是说：“别担心那200,000美元了，你们不必还了。”

00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:45.460
<v Samir Noriega>Yeah, that was forgiven. This was deferred.
<v Samir Noriega>对，那笔钱被免除了。这笔被延期了。

00:49:45.460 --> 00:49:48.020
<v David Kestenbaum>They forgave $200,000?
<v David Kestenbaum>他们免除了200,000美元？

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:48.960
<v Samir Noriega>That's what it looks like.
<v Samir Noriega>看起来是这样的。

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:55.090
<v David Kestenbaum>What're they doing? I mean, I feel for that person, but that was money we were supposed to be getting some of.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们在搞什么？我的意思是，我对那个人感同身受，但那是我们本该分到一部分的钱。

00:49:55.090 --> 00:50:06.940
<v Steve Marcus>In essence, because of their forgiving that homeowner that principal balance, it's affecting Toxie. So their forgiving is at your expense.
<v Steve Marcus>本质上，由于他们免除了那个房主的本金余额，它正在影响托克西。所以他们的免除是以牺牲你们的利益为代价的。

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:27.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Loan modification. This was a real surprise to me, because usually you hear, number one, how loan modifications aren't happening enough, and number two, how they're good for everyone. If you modify a loan, you can keep a family in their home-- that's good for them-- and it's good for the lender, who doesn't have to foreclose on the house and sell it for what could be an even bigger loss.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>贷款修改。这对我来说是个真正的惊喜，因为通常你会听到，第一，贷款修改做得还不够多；第二，它们对每个人都有好处。如果你修改了一笔贷款，你就可以让一个家庭留在他们的房子里——这对他们有好处——而且对贷款机构也有好处，贷款机构不必对房屋进行止赎并以可能造成更大损失的价格将其卖掉。

00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:58.420
<v David Kestenbaum>But there is one group of people it is not good for-- us. And this is where, I'm afraid, we have to dig into the toxic asset paperwork. We have to explain the line. So the way these mortgage backed securities work is that us and all the other investors who collectively own these 2,000 mortgages, we are basically standing in line to get paid. Every month, money comes in from people's mortgage checks, but that money first gets used to pay the investors standing in front of the line, then the people behind them, then the people behind them.
<v David Kestenbaum>但有一群人它是没有好处的——我们。而这就是我恐慌的地方，我们必须深入研究有毒资产的文件。我们必须解释这一行。这些抵押贷款支持证券的运作方式是，我们以及共同拥有这2,000笔抵押贷款的所有其他投资者，我们基本上是在排队等待付款。每个月，人们的抵押贷款支票都有资金流入，但这些资金首先被用来支付排在队伍最前面的投资者，然后是他们后面的人，再后面的人。

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:22.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And we are standing way at the back of the line. And that's why our slice of this thing is toxic, that's why we got it so cheap. The people in the front paid more than us. So loan modification's bad for us. This Brentwood, California guy, he's got a smaller mortgage now, which means he's writing a smaller mortgage check each month, which means that pile of mortgages is just a little bit smaller, with less money coming in.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而我们排在队伍的最末端。这就是为什么我们切到的这块蛋糕是有毒的，这就是为什么我们能以如此便宜的价格买到它。前面的人付的钱比我们多。所以贷款修改对我们是不利的。这个加州布伦特伍德的家伙，他现在的房贷变小了，这意味着他每个月开出的房贷支票变小了，这意味着那一堆抵押贷款变小了一点点，流进来的钱也变少了。

00:51:22.320 --> 00:51:31.130
<v David Kestenbaum>This month, just six mortgages in Toxie got modified, but that was enough so that the money ran out just before it got to us, at the last place in line.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个月，托克西里只有六笔贷款被修改了，但这就足够让资金在刚流到我们这里之前就用光了——也就是队伍里的最后一个位置。

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:46.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is one reason why it's been difficult to arrange loan work outs, to help people who can no longer make their payments, because there's a whole bunch of people like us who lose money when you do. I ask Samir, do investors like us call the bank and say, "Stop doing these modifications, you're killing me."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是为什么很难安排贷款重组、去帮助那些无力还款的人的原因之一，因为有一大群像我们这样的人，当你这么做时会亏钱。我问萨米尔，像我们这样的投资者会打电话给银行说“别再做这些修改了，你们在害我”吗？

00:51:46.480 --> 00:51:47.730
<v Samir Noriega>You could do that.
<v Samir Noriega>你可以这么做。

00:51:47.730 --> 00:51:49.160
<v David Kestenbaum>Would you do that if you owned this?
<v David Kestenbaum>如果你拥有这些资产，你会这么做吗？

00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:53.820
<v Samir Noriega>I own some of them, so I do that, but they don't care.
<v Samir Noriega>我拥有其中的一些，所以我会这么做，但他们根本不在乎。

00:51:53.820 --> 00:52:09.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They might care, but there's a lot of pressure the other way. Some investors at the front of the line want the banks to do modifications, because any money that comes in goes to them first. The government wants banks to do modifications and has programs to encourage it, and homeowners want them, too.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们可能会在乎，但有来自另一个方向的巨大压力。排在队伍前列的一些投资者希望银行进行修改，因为任何进账的钱首先都会归他们。政府希望银行进行修改并有相应的计划来鼓励这样做，房主们也希望如此。

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:17.620
<v David Kestenbaum>So when Samir is looking at Toxie's numbers on the screen, he's pretty pessimistic. In fact, there's only one way that things are going to turn around.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以当萨米尔看着屏幕上的托克西的数字时，他非常悲观。事实上，事情要好转只有一种途径。

00:52:17.620 --> 00:52:32.400
<v Samir Noriega>If everyone won the lottery in this pool, however unlikely that is, and they all pay their mortgage entirely, you'd be getting close to $75,000.
<v Samir Noriega>如果这个池子里的每个人都中了彩票——尽管这极其不可能——并且他们全部都足额支付了抵押贷款，你们将能拿到接近75,000美元。

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:38.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Excellent. Then Toxie would be alive and healthy again.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>太棒了。那托克西就又能活过来、恢复健康了。

00:52:38.020 --> 00:52:55.350
<v Samir Noriega>It's still alive. As long as you have an outstanding principal balance, there's hope, but it's fading very quickly.
<v Samir Noriega>它仍然活着。只要你还有未偿还的本金余额，就还有希望，但希望正在迅速消退。

00:52:55.350 --> 00:53:04.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>September 19, 2010. It was pretty clear where we were headed. I went through blame, anger, denial, and then we got the call.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>2010年9月19日。我们前进的方向已经相当明确了。我经历了责备、愤怒、否定，然后我们接到了电话。

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:11.740
<v Wit Solberg>Well, the day has come. Toxie has officially died.
<v Wit Solberg>好吧，这一天还是来了。托克西正式死亡了。

00:53:11.740 --> 00:53:12.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's over?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>结束了？

00:53:12.990 --> 00:53:15.030
<v Wit Solberg>It is over.
<v Wit Solberg>结束了。

00:53:15.030 --> 00:53:19.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That means there's no chance we're getting any more checks?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这意味着我们再也没有机会拿到任何支票了？

00:53:19.020 --> 00:53:22.770
<v Wit Solberg>She's dead, Chana. She's not coming back.
<v Wit Solberg>她死了，查娜（Chana）。她回不来了。

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:30.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We'd bought Toxie for $1,000, and at the time of death, we had made back $449.06.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们用1000美元买下了托克西，而在她死亡之时，我们收回了449.06美元。

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:54.920
<v David Kestenbaum>In trying to find comfort in that moment, I console myself with this thought-- with Toxie's demise, we are one step closer to the end of this financial crisis. Now we have an answer to that big question, what are toxic assets worth? The question that's vexed governments, and big banks, and the world's finance ministers-- at least for our one toxic asset, we now know the answer-- she was worth $449.06.
<v David Kestenbaum>为了在那一刻寻找慰藉，我用这样的想法来安慰自己——随着托克西的消亡，我们距离这场金融危机的结束又近了一步。现在我们有了那个宏大问题的答案：有毒资产到底值多少钱？这个问题一直困扰着政府、大银行和世界的财政部长们——至少对于我们的一项有毒资产而言，我们现在知道了答案——她价值449.06美元。

00:53:54.920 --> 00:54:02.120
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Although Wit, who sees these things traded every day-- billions of dollars worth-- did not feel like this meant closure.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>尽管每天都看到这些资产被交易——价值数十亿美元——的维特，并不觉得这意味着尘埃落定。

00:54:02.120 --> 00:54:17.300
<v Wit Solberg>We're in like the third inning, if you will, on the toxic asset business. There's still a lot more that's sitting in people's balance sheets, you know, waiting for the day that they're able to sell. They're not going away anytime soon.
<v Wit Solberg>如果你愿意的话，我们在有毒资产业务上就像刚进入第三局。还有更多资产正躺在人们的资产负债表上，你知道的，在等待着他们能够出售的那一天。它们不会很快消失的。

00:54:17.300 --> 00:54:39.520
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In other words, Toxie is survived by millions of investors and homeowners, who are part of some other toxic asset story. We still don't know how those stories are going to end. The economy's more stable than it was, but housing is still a big, big unknown thing.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>换句话说，Toxie留下的遗产是数百万投资者和房主，他们是另一个有毒资产故事的一部分。我们仍然不知道这些故事将如何结局。经济比过去更稳定了，但房地产仍然是一个巨大、未知的变数。

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:50.770
<v David Kestenbaum>I turned off the live video cam pointing at the stack of paper that was Toxie. And Chana, you scattered the remains. And by that I mean you handed out the money we got back from Toxie to our colleagues at Planet Money.
<v David Kestenbaum>我关掉了对着那一叠代表Toxie的纸张的实时网络摄像头。查娜，你撒了骨灰。我是指你把从Toxie那里收回的钱分给了我们《金钱星球》的同事们。

00:54:50.770 --> 00:54:56.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>85, 86, 87, 88, 89.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>85, 86, 87, 88, 89.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:55:00.170
<v Adam Davidson>So this is it? This is what Toxie has come down to?
<v Adam Davidson>所以这就结束了？这就是Toxie的最终下场吗？

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:03.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Oh no, wait, here.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>噢不，等等，给你。

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:06.780
<v Adam Davidson>She brings out the change.
<v Adam Davidson>她拿出了零钱。

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And 80 cents, here you go.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>还有80美分，给你。

00:55:09.330 --> 00:55:10.520
<v Adam Davidson>This is so depressing.
<v Adam Davidson>这太让人沮丧了。

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:13.580
<v Caitlin Kenney>Wait, does that mean we're all only getting that much back?
<v Caitlin Kenney>等等，这难道意味着我们每个人只能拿回这么多钱吗？

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:15.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>$89.80.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>$89.80.

00:55:15.570 --> 00:55:18.430
<v David Kestenbaum>We turned your $200 into $89.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们把你的200美元变成89美元。

00:55:18.430 --> 00:55:21.990
<v Caitlin Kenney>It's really depressing that this is all that's left of Toxie.
<v Caitlin Kenney>Toxie只剩下这些，真的让人很沮丧。

00:55:21.990 --> 00:55:23.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就是这样。

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:25.150
<v Jacob Goldstein>So I've been sitting here lurking--
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以我一直坐在这里旁观——

00:55:25.150 --> 00:55:33.810
<v David Kestenbaum>That's Planet Money blogger Jacob Goldstein, who wasn't part of the staff when we bought Toxie. And he was sitting in the room, watching us each get our $89.80.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是《金钱星球》的博客作者雅各布·戈尔茨坦，在我们买下Toxie时他還不是员工。他当时坐在房间里，看着我们每个人领回89.80美元。

00:55:33.810 --> 00:55:38.580
<v Jacob Goldstein>So look, I have a proposition, basically. It's very safe.
<v Jacob Goldstein>听着，基本上我有个提议。非常安全。

00:55:38.580 --> 00:55:39.710
<v Caitlin Kenney>Sounds familiar.
<v Caitlin Kenney>听起来很耳熟。

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:59.960
<v Jacob Goldstein>And it involves you giving me $89.80, each of you. You have it there sitting in front of you. Adam, you already said it doesn't feel like a lot of money. I want you to take that money right now, put it in my hand, because I have-- what for all of history has been-- the very definition of a sound investment. Gold.
<v Jacob Goldstein>这需要你们每个人把89.80美元交给我。你们就把它放在面前。亚当，你刚才已经说过这感觉没多少钱。我希望你们现在就把这钱拿出来，放到我手里，因为我有——纵观整个历史——这堪称稳健投资的代名词。黄金。

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:00.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Gold?
<v David Kestenbaum>黄金？

00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:07.210
<v Jacob Goldstein>One word. We're going to buy whatever, a penny size piece of gold, that will be our rock.
<v Jacob Goldstein>就一个词。我们要买个什么东西，一块硬币大小的金子，那将是我们的镇宅之宝。

00:56:07.210 --> 00:56:09.600
<v David Kestenbaum>And you're going to bring in that gold, we're going to see the gold?
<v David Kestenbaum>然后你要把那块金子拿来，我们要看那块金子吗？

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:13.250
<v Jacob Goldstein>We are going to have what you call a gold.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我们会拥有你们所说的黄金。

00:56:13.250 --> 00:56:35.120
<v David Kestenbaum>On October 4, Jacob and I went to the diamond district in New York City with Toxie's remains in a mason jar, and we bought a shiny gold coin. It cost $419, twice as much as it would have cost three years ago, which means that yes, it might be a bubble. But this time, if we lose money, at least we got in on the way up instead of the way down.
<v David Kestenbaum>10月4日，我和雅各布带着装在梅森罐里的Toxie的骨灰去了纽约市的钻石区，买了一枚闪亮的金币。它花了419美元，是三年前价格的两倍，这意味着，是的，这可能是一个泡沫。但这一次，如果我们亏钱，至少我们是在价格上涨时入场的，而不是在下跌时。

00:56:35.120 --> 00:56:42.000
